{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"notorious-deadsmooth-rattail","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Context / Problem\nAfter the sibling story that adds `verify.trusted_opcert_keys` and sets it in the MCP deployment, the server verifies op-certs against its own list. The rest of rebar still reads the repo-pinned `.rebar/trusted_environments.yaml`: the verify trust root (`_opcert_trust_root`, `_pinned_environment_keys`, `_rotated_own_key_verdict` in `src/rebar/_opcert_verify.py`), the signing refusal (`_pinned_public_key_bodies`, `_refuse_if_pinned_principal_key_mismatch` in `src/rebar/_opcert_signing.py`, called at line 366), the health fallback in `src/rebar/_mcp_opcert_health.py`, and the merge gate `rebar verify-opcert` (`src/rebar/_commands/verify_opcert.py`, calling `trusted_env.verify_required_environment` at line 192). Per ADR 0125 the default is validity-only and signing is never gated on an allowed key. This story stops every code path reading the pin file; the next sibling deletes the file and command. Sequencing: built on the trust-list story's merged commit.\n\n## Approach\n1. `src/rebar/_opcert_verify.py`: no list means the trust root is the SSHSIG's embedded key for the certificate's principal (trust basis `envelope_key`); a list applies the existing list check (`trust_list` or `untrusted`). Delete `_opcert_trust_root`, `_pinned_environment_keys`, `_rotated_own_key_verdict`; rotation needs no special case because the embedded key verifies its own signature. `verify.require_environment` set requires principal equality AND a list, else `foreign_key` with a reason saying a list is required. Results keep `signing_key` and `key_id` (the environment).\n2. `src/rebar/_opcert_signing.py`: delete `_pinned_public_key_bodies`, `_refuse_if_pinned_principal_key_mismatch` and its call, so signing never consults trust configuration. Delete `_warn_if_verify_cannot_resolve` and `_opcert_own_public_keys`: the warning exists only because the old verify path trusted the environment's own key files, which no verify path uses afterwards (confirm with Serena references plus `git grep -nF` per name before deleting).\n3. `src/rebar/_mcp_opcert_health.py`: drop the pin-file branch; `expected` is true only when the list is set.\n4. `src/rebar/_commands/verify_opcert.py`: verify each closed ticket's completion-verifier certificate with a new helper `verify_completion_opcert(envelope, bound, ticket_id, material, merged_commit, *, required_env, trusted_keys)` in `src/rebar/_opcert_verify.py`: principal equals `required_env`, (principal, key) listed, signature verifies under that key, and the signed subject digest equals `rebar.attest.opcert.opcert_subject_digest(ticket_id, material, merged_commit, \"completion-verifier\")`. Keep the authoritative-material recompute and the merged-commit ancestry check; remove the storage-anchor lookup (`_opcert_anchor_event`) and era arguments, which served only pin-file key eras. Required environment without a list exits 2 naming both keys. Move the per-ticket check into a helper so `cli` stays at or below its complexity ceiling of 20. The imports of `_collect_all`, `_is_enforced`, `_resolve_commit` from `verify_authorship.py` stay unchanged (that file belongs to starving-sphalerite-skua and is not edited).\n5. `trust_basis` becomes `envelope_key | trust_list | null` in `src/rebar/schemas/verify_signature_result.schema.json` and `src/rebar/types.py`; update help in `src/rebar/_cli/_parsers/advanced/verify.py` and `src/rebar/_cli/help/verify-opcert.txt`; regenerate `docs/cli-reference.md`.\n6. Rewrite op-cert tests that build pin files to use the list or validity-only; delete modules that test only removed behaviour.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/_opcert_verify.py`, `src/rebar/_opcert_signing.py`, `src/rebar/_mcp_opcert_health.py`, `src/rebar/_commands/verify_opcert.py`\n- `src/rebar/schemas/verify_signature_result.schema.json`, `src/rebar/types.py`, `src/rebar/_cli/_parsers/advanced/verify.py`, `src/rebar/_cli/help/verify-opcert.txt`, `docs/cli-reference.md`\n- tests edited: `tests/unit/test_opcert_trust_list.py`, `tests/unit/test_opcert_cross_environment_c21f.py`, `tests/unit/test_verify_opcert.py`, `tests/unit/test_verify_opcert_ac_coverage.py`, `tests/unit/test_mcp_opcert_signing_goose.py`, `tests/unit/test_opcert_binding_thread_propagation_ff4a.py`, `tests/unit/test_opcert_rollback.py`, `tests/unit/test_mcp_plan_review_status.py`, `tests/unit/test_bugfix_size_gate_ad0d.py`, `tests/unit/test_opcert_own_pub_resolution_d26a.py`, `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_signature.py`\n- tests deleted: `tests/unit/test_opcert_own_principal_rotation_d909.py`, `tests/unit/test_opcert_public_counterpart_guard_879b.py`\nOut of scope: deleting the pin file, `src/rebar/attest/trusted_env.py` and `rebar trusted-env` (next sibling); prose docs and runbooks (docs sibling). `rebar.attest.opcert.verify_opcert` stays as a library primitive.\n\n## Testing\nNo list: a validly signed certificate from any key is `certified` with `envelope_key`, `signing_key` and `key_id`; tampered payload, cross-ticket or cross-kind replay is `mismatch`; undecodable envelope is `invalid`; a certificate under a rotated-out own key verifies. List set: listed passes, unlisted is `untrusted`. `require_environment` without a list gives `foreign_key`; with a list and a listed key of that environment, `certified`. Signing succeeds for a key on no list. `verify-opcert`: listed completion cert passes; unlisted fails exit 1; required environment without a list exits 2; no required environment is advisory exit 0. Health: list unset gives `expected` false.\nPortability: removes code; remaining checks use the existing `ssh-keygen` path; no new subprocess, fd, path or permission handling.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] With no trust setting, op-cert verification passes a validly signed certificate from any key, fails a tampered or wrongly bound certificate, and reports `signing_key` and the environment (checked: `pytest tests/unit/test_opcert_trust_list.py tests/unit/test_opcert_cross_environment_c21f.py -q`).\n- [ ] Signing succeeds for a key that is on no list (checked: a test in `tests/unit/test_mcp_opcert_signing_goose.py`).\n- [ ] `rebar verify-opcert` verifies completion certificates against the trust list with no pin file (checked: `pytest tests/unit/test_verify_opcert.py tests/unit/test_verify_opcert_ac_coverage.py -q`).\n- [ ] `git grep -nF trusted_env -- src/rebar/_opcert_verify.py src/rebar/_opcert_signing.py src/rebar/_mcp_opcert_health.py src/rebar/_commands/verify_opcert.py` prints no hits.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] After autodeploy rolls this change, the live MCP `/health` `opcert` field still reads `expected: true`, `matched: true` and no `error`.\n  provenance: environment=rebar Gerrit box i-00880b2c7f13527c5; principal=operator AWS profile frontier; privilege_posture=read-only SSM command running curl against the MCP loopback /health; instrument=aws ssm send-command + get-command-invocation — the health route is loopback-only, so no repository check can observe the deployed container.\n- [ ] `make verify` is green, and the change lands through Gerrit with LLM-Review +1 and Verified +1.","id":"5220-052f-6d9c-4699","parent_id":"0dd0-cecb-3932-4c90","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Op-cert verification is validity-only by default; no code path reads the repo-pinned trust file"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1795112637875527528,"uuid":"7abae9c2-c88c-410a-ad93-79cb355e868d"}